# Licensing

VayuWeb is licensed in two layers, because the charter and the code need different things from
a licence. Full texts are in [`LICENSES/`](LICENSES).

| Layer | What it covers | Licence |
|---|---|---|
| **The charter** | `constitution/` | **CC0-1.0** — public domain dedication |
| **The specifications** | `docs/` | **CC0-1.0** — public domain dedication |
| **Brand artwork** | `assets/` | **CC0-1.0** — public domain dedication |
| **Code** | `registry/`, `proxy/`, `client/`, `scripts/` | **MIT** |

Article 16.2 of the Constitution governs this and is not optional: specifications must be
published under "permissive or public-domain-equivalent terms", and reference code under "the
MIT licence or terms no less permissive".

---

## Why the charter and specifications are public domain

Anyone must be able to implement VayuWeb without asking permission, without attribution
obligations, and without a lawyer reading a licence first. Article 16 makes independent
implementation a right; a licence on the specification would make it a privilege.

It matters for forking too. Article 17 guarantees the right to fork the protocol, the registry
state and the name, and Article 17.2 forbids any anti-fork clause in any licence. A fork must be
able to carry the charter away intact. **A licence on a founding document is a leash on a fork**,
and the right to fork is the last check that survives after every other one has failed.

CC0 rather than a permissive licence because even an attribution requirement is a condition, and
a condition is something a future party could litigate.

## Why the code is MIT

Because Article 16.2 requires it. That is the whole reason, and it is a sufficient one.

There is a live argument that it should not, and it is being made properly rather than by
editing this file — see **[VWIP-0002](docs/spec/VWIP-0002.md)**, which proposes amending
Article 16.2 to permit reciprocal licensing of the reference implementation. Until that proposal
is ratified, this file states what the Constitution presently requires.

If you want to know the argument before reading the proposal: MIT permits a well-capitalised
operator to take this code, improve it privately, run the improved version as a closed hosted
service, and contribute nothing back. Reciprocal licensing would require those improvements to
be published. Whether that trade is worth making is a governance question, not a preference, and
Article 16 is amendable precisely so it can be argued.

## Contributions: DCO, and no CLA — ever

Contributions are accepted under the **Developer Certificate of Origin**, signed off per commit,
as the Linux kernel does. See [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md).

**There is no Contributor Licence Agreement and there will never be one.** This is the most
important sentence in this file.

A CLA concentrates copyright in a single party. Only a party that holds the copyright can sell
proprietary licences to a codebase, so a CLA is what makes a commercial dual-licence business
constructible — the free version under one licence, and paid proprietary terms with a warranty
and an indemnity for anyone who wants them. Every well-known conversion of a community project
into a paid product was built on rights a CLA had gathered first.

Without a CLA that cannot be built here. Not by a future maintainer, not by an acquirer, and not
by the author. There is no way to warrant title over code you do not own.

Be precise about what this does **not** mean, because the usual summary overstates it. It does not
freeze the outbound licence: MIT permits distributing a derivative work under different terms, so
a future version of this code could be released under a reciprocal licence without anyone's
consent. That is what permissive licensing is for, and it is why VWIP-0002 is a policy question
rather than a legal impossibility. The comparison sometimes drawn to the Linux kernel does not
carry over — GPL-2.0-only genuinely cannot be sublicensed under other terms, and MIT genuinely
can.

What no party can ever do is **retract a grant already made**. Every release published under
these terms stays under them permanently, forkable from its tag whatever this repository does
afterwards. That guarantee protects anyone who has already relied on this work, and it does not
depend on anybody's continued goodwill.

So the commitment on top of the licence, which is a promise rather than a legal constraint: any
change to the outbound licence will be **forward-only, announced before it happens, and put to
contributors first** even where their agreement is not required. See
**[VWIP-0002](docs/spec/VWIP-0002.md)**, which is where that argument belongs.

---

## Copyright

Copyright the VayuWeb contributors.

Copyright is held by each contributor over their own work. It is not assigned to any person or
entity, deliberately, for the reason given above.

## SPDX summary

```text
constitution/**              CC0-1.0
docs/**                      CC0-1.0
assets/**                    CC0-1.0
registry/**                  MIT
proxy/**                     MIT
client/**                    MIT
scripts/**                   MIT
```

Brand artwork is dedicated to the public domain as a matter of *licence*. That is separate from
trademark: Article 54 governs use of the project's name and marks, and a public-domain image does
not confer permission to present a fork as though it were VayuWeb.

